(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["clinical-medicine-and-surgery-i-exam-1-cms-exam-1-master-exam-updated-form-a"]={"title":"CMS I Exam 1 Master Exam (Updated) — Form A","category":"Clinical Medicine and Surgery I","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"A 63-year-old man presents with a lesion on the temple that he is unable to describe, and the clinician has not yet formed an impression. What is the recommended first step when evaluating any premalignant or malignant cutaneous lesion?","choices":["Characterise the lesion before naming it","Name the likely diagnosis, then seek support","Photograph and review next visit","Refer every pigmented lesion","Biopsy every lesion first"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The foundational approach is to describe before naming: characterise each lesion systematically by primary lesion type, colour, surface texture, border definition, size, distribution, palpability, ulceration, and bleeding before assigning a diagnosis. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 4"},{"q":"A 31-year-old man of Northern European ancestry presents with an intensely pruritic, burning eruption of grouped small vesicles and papules symmetrically distributed over the elbows, knees, and buttocks. Most lesions are excoriated. He has autoimmune thyroid disease. What direct immunofluorescence finding is expected on perilesional skin?","choices":["Linear immunoglobulin G at the basement membrane","Band-like lymphocytes in the upper dermis","No deposits; mites on scraping","Net-like intercellular immunoglobulin G","Granular immunoglobulin A in dermal papillae"],"answer":4,"exp":"Correct. Dermatitis herpetiformis is caused by immunoglobulin A antibodies against epidermal transglutaminase in genetically susceptible individuals consuming gluten. The immune complexes deposit in the dermal papillae, and perilesional direct immunofluorescence showing granular immunoglobulin A is the gold standard test. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 125"},{"q":"A 39-year-old man has a moveable, round, firm nodule on the back with a visible central pore, which occasionally discharges foul-smelling material. It is currently red, warm, and tender. What is the appropriate management?","choices":["Aspirate and discharge","Leave it alone permanently","Delay excision; inject triamcinolone now","Urgent wide excision","Excise the capsule now while inflamed"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. If the cyst is inflamed, excision should be postponed for a few weeks, inflammation reduced with an intralesional steroid injection of triamcinolone, and antibiotics given if needed. Surgical removal of the entire capsule is performed when the cyst is not inflamed. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 56"},{"q":"A 27-year-old woman has folliculitis of the thighs that has failed to respond to antibacterial treatment, and a hair is plucked from an affected follicle. What test should be performed on it?","choices":["Direct immunofluorescence","A mineral oil preparation","A potassium hydroxide wet mount","A Wood's lamp examination","A Tzanck smear"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. In resistant cases a potassium hydroxide wet mount using a plucked hair is used to rule out fungal, specifically dermatophyte, folliculitis. Culture and Gram stain from an unroofed pustule are also performed. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 44"},{"q":"A 58-year-old man taking allopurinol for three weeks develops fever, malaise, and painful swallowing, followed by erosions of the lips, mouth, and conjunctivae, and dusky skin lesions with detachment involving about 6 percent of his body surface area. What is the single most important immediate action?","choices":["Give systemic corticosteroids","Start broad-spectrum antibiotics","Withdraw all suspect medications","Obtain a skin biopsy","Admit to a burn unit"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Immediate causative drug withdrawal is the first step, and earlier withdrawal is strongly associated with improved survival — each day of delay worsens prognosis. Allopurinol is named as the most common trigger worldwide. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 83"},{"q":"A 66-year-old woman has a slow-growing 1 cm papule on the nose with a pearly translucent quality and visible telangiectasias that become more apparent when the skin is stretched. There is central erosion. What subtype is this?","choices":["Keratoacanthoma","Nodular squamous cell carcinoma","Nodular basal cell carcinoma","Sebaceous hyperplasia","Amelanotic melanoma"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Nodular basal cell carcinoma is a papule or nodule with central erosion, growing slowly over years to 1 to 2 cm or larger, with a pearly or translucent quality and visible telangiectasias accentuated by stretching the skin. Basal cell carcinoma is the most common form of cancer, driven by ultraviolet light in fair-skinned people with intense intermittent exposure. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 35"},{"q":"A 5-year-old boy is diagnosed with varicella and his parents ask when he may return to school. For how long is a child with varicella considered contagious?","choices":["Only while vesicles are present, ending once any crusting begins","From the appearance of the rash until all lesions have crusted","For 21 days from exposure regardless of the rash","From 1 to 2 days before the rash until all lesions have crusted","Until fever resolves, regardless of the state of the lesions"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Patients are contagious from 1 to 2 days before the rash appears until all lesions crust. In breakthrough disease without crusts, the period runs until no new lesions have appeared for 24 hours. — 6. Fungal and Viral Skin Infections - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 89"},{"q":"A 19-year-old man presents two days after clearing brush at a summer job. He reports intense itching. Examination shows erythematous papules, vesicles, and a few small bullae arranged in linear streaks across the forearms and one calf, with excoriation between them. Some lesions appear newer than others. He is afebrile and the rash does not cross the midline of the trunk or follow a single band. What feature of the eruption best supports the diagnosis?","choices":["The intensity of the pruritus","The presence of vesicles and bullae","The linear arrangement of the lesions","The onset within two days of the exposure","The presence of excoriation between lesions"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Urushiol sap from Toxicodendron species produces intensely pruritic vesicular lesions in linear distributions, because the streaks mark where the plant grazed the skin in passing. Lesions erupt within 4 to 96 hours and may appear in multiple stages, as they do here. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 87"},{"q":"A 29-year-old Black man with pseudofolliculitis barbae cannot stop shaving because his employer requires a clean-shaven face. What advice about shaving technique is most appropriate?","choices":["Avoid lift-and-cut razors; use a single blade","Keep shaving; rely on a topical steroid","Use a multi-blade lift-and-cut razor","Shave daily against the grain","Use a chemical depilatory on shaved skin"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Patients should avoid lift-and-cut razor systems and look for mild razor angles with single or at most double blades, because those systems cut the hair below the skin surface where it can re-enter the follicular wall. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 57"},{"q":"A 28-year-old woman has multiple smooth, slightly elevated, flat-topped, light-brown papules across her forehead and dorsal hands. She reports they seemed to multiply along the line where she shaves her legs. What form is this, and what advice follows?","choices":["Verruca vulgaris; salicylic acid to each","Seborrheic keratoses; no treatment","Flat warts; observe, as they resolve spontaneously","Molluscum contagiosum; cantharidin","Plantar warts; salicylic acid and cryotherapy"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Flat warts are multiple smooth, slightly elevated, flat-topped, skin-coloured to light-brown papules common on the face, forehead, dorsal hands, and shins, and shaving can spread lesions through autoinoculation. Observation is reasonable because spontaneous resolution is likely. — 6. Fungal and Viral Skin Infections - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 143"},{"q":"A 66-year-old man has a biopsy-proven superficial basal cell carcinoma on the trunk with no high-risk features and would prefer to avoid surgery. For which basal cell carcinoma is topical therapy an appropriate option, and what must follow it?","choices":["Selected superficial disease, clearance confirmed","High-risk facial tumours","Any tumour the patient wants left alone","All nodular tumours, no follow-up","Recurrent tumours after excision"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Topical therapy is used for selected superficial basal cell carcinoma — imiquimod five nights weekly for 6 to 10 weeks or fluorouracil twice daily for up to 12 weeks — and clinical clearance must be confirmed after treatment. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 37"},{"q":"A 15-year-old boy presents with facial lesions. Examination shows open and closed comedones across the forehead and nose with no papules, pustules, or nodules. What is the most appropriate first-line treatment?","choices":["A topical retinoid","A topical retinoid combined with an oral antibiotic","An oral contraceptive pill","Topical benzoyl peroxide alone","Oral isotretinoin as monotherapy"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. For comedonal, non-inflammatory acne the guideline is a topical retinoid, which is comedolytic and anti-inflammatory and normalises the follicular hyperkeratinisation. If it is not tolerated, azelaic acid or salicylic acid may be substituted. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 32"},{"q":"A 27-year-old woman presents with burning and small erythematous papules around her mouth. Examination demonstrates grouped monomorphic papules and papulopustules involving the nasolabial folds and chin, with a narrow zone of sparing immediately adjacent to the vermilion border. There are no open or closed comedones. She has applied hydrocortisone cream to her face daily for several months. What is the most appropriate initial management?","choices":["Add adapalene, continue the steroid","Oral isotretinoin","Stop the steroid; expect no flare","Stop the steroid; warn of a temporary flare","Increase the steroid potency"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Topical corticosteroid exposure is the most important modifiable association, so facial steroids should be discontinued because continued exposure perpetuates the condition. Patients must be told the eruption may temporarily worsen after withdrawal, or they will restart the steroid when it does. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 102"},{"q":"A 61-year-old man with diabetes has a lesion at the site of a recent insect bite that began as a pustule over inflamed skin and deepened into a dermal ulceration covered by a thick grey-yellow crust. What form of impetigo is this?","choices":["Bullous impetigo","Ecthyma","Erysipelas","Non-bullous impetigo","Cellulitis"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Ecthyma is uncommon and is predisposed to by pre-existing tissue damage such as bites and by immunocompromised states such as diabetes. It begins as a vesicle or pustule over inflamed skin that deepens into dermal ulceration with a thicker grey-yellow crust. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 91"},{"q":"A 5-year-old boy has several light brown macules on the trunk, and his 30-year-old father has similar but larger ones. What size threshold defines a café au lait macule as significant before and after puberty?","choices":["Over 5 mm before puberty, over 15 mm after","Over 5 mm at any age","Size is not part of the definition","Over 1 cm at any age","Over 15 mm before puberty, over 5 mm after"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Café au lait spots are light tan to brown macules greater than 5 mm prepubertally and greater than 15 mm postpubertally. They are often the first manifestation of neurofibromatosis type 1 and are usually present at birth or appear during the first year. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 99"},{"q":"A 24-year-old woman has symmetrical, well-defined white non-scaly macules and patches on the dorsal hands and around the mouth. They fluoresce under a Wood's lamp. What is the underlying mechanism?","choices":["Postinflammatory loss of pigment after a dermatitis","T cell mediated autoimmune destruction of melanocytes","Proliferation of basal melanocytes with increased melanin","Reduced melanin production by intact melanocytes","Overgrowth of Malassezia altering melanocyte function"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Vitiligo is a common autoimmune disease causing depigmentation through T cell mediated destruction of melanocytes. It can begin at any age but usually starts before the thirties, with half of patients presenting before their twenties. — CMS I Pigmented Skin Lesions - Shahsv-2.pptx, Slide 21"},{"q":"A 45-year-old dishwasher has had swollen, tender nail folds on several fingers for four months, without fluctuance. The nail plates are thickened and discoloured and the cuticles have separated from the nail plate. What is the most common pathogen, and what is the key patient instruction?","choices":["Candida albicans; keep the hands dry","A dermatophyte; oral terbinafine","Pseudomonas aeruginosa; remove the nail","Staphylococcus aureus; keep the hands dry","Candida albicans; soak the hands daily"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Chronic paronychia is an inflammatory reaction of the proximal nail fold to irritants and allergens, and Candida albicans is the most common pathogen. Predisposing occupations include dishwashers, bartenders, cleaners, and cooks, and patients should be instructed to keep the hands as dry as possible. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 123"},{"q":"A 54-year-old man with non-purulent cellulitis of the leg is started on cephalexin. Forty-eight hours later he still has fever and the affected area has extended. What should be considered?","choices":["Deep vein thrombosis alone","Contact dermatitis","A normal first-day treatment response","Allergy to cephalexin","Necrotizing fasciitis"],"answer":4,"exp":"Correct. The differential specifically states that necrotizing fasciitis should be considered if there is no response to antibiotics within 48 hours. It may initially be diagnosed as cellulitis, with the patient sent home and returning worse. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 108"},{"q":"A 6-year-old girl with varicella is febrile and uncomfortable, and her father asks what he may give her. What analgesic caution applies?","choices":["Avoid all analgesics","Avoid aspirin; use caution with anti-inflammatories","Give systemic corticosteroids","Give antivirals instead of analgesia","Avoid acetaminophen; prefer aspirin"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Management is supportive, avoiding aspirin in children and using caution with non-steroidal anti-inflammatory drugs. Early oral antivirals are considered for higher-risk patients and intravenous acyclovir for severe disease. — 6. Fungal and Viral Skin Infections - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 87"},{"q":"A 58-year-old woman with obesity has candidal intertrigo of both inframammary folds. Beyond antifungal therapy, what environmental measure is central to treatment?","choices":["Apply an occlusive ointment to the folds","Bind the folds tightly together","Dry the folds and reduce friction and occlusion","Apply a high-potency corticosteroid","Increase the frequency of hot showers"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Correcting the environment is central: gently dry the folds, reduce friction and occlusion, use moisture-wicking fabric or absorbent material, and address incontinence or hyperhidrosis. Intertrigo itself is caused by friction, moisture, and heat trapped in body folds. — 6. Fungal and Viral Skin Infections - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 72"},{"q":"A 55-year-old man is admitted with widespread painful erythema progressing to flaccid bullae and confluent epidermal detachment of about 40 percent of the body surface area, with a positive Nikolsky sign and severe mucosal erosions. What distinguishes this from Stevens-Johnson syndrome?","choices":["Mucosal involvement","A positive Nikolsky sign","A drug trigger","Detachment over 30 percent of body surface","A prodrome"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The two conditions sit on one spectrum and are separated by the percentage of body surface area involved: Stevens-Johnson syndrome has less than 10 percent detachment, and toxic epidermal necrolysis has more than 30 percent, with mortality up to 30 to 35 percent. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 88"},{"q":"A 72-year-old woman has more than twenty rough scaly papules scattered across a bald, sun-damaged scalp, with no single lesion standing out from the rest. When is field-directed rather than lesion-directed therapy indicated for actinic keratoses?","choices":["When multiple lesions occupy a field of sun-damaged skin","Only after cryotherapy has failed twice","Only in immunosuppressed patients","When a single lesion has clear borders","When the lesion is thick, indurated, and bleeding"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Field-directed therapy is indicated when multiple lesions occupy a field of sun-damaged skin, reflecting that chronic ultraviolet injury produces dysplastic change across a field rather than in isolated spots. Lesion-directed cryotherapy is preferred for isolated or few lesions with clear borders. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 14"},{"q":"A 24-year-old woman is given silicone gel sheeting for a keloid on the chest and asks how it works and how long she must wear it. What is the theoretical basis for using it, and how long is it worn?","choices":["It cools the scar; worn for two weeks","It warms the scar, raising collagenase; worn up to a year","It blocks ultraviolet; worn outdoors","It delivers steroid; worn for six weeks","It induces hypoxia; worn for one month"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The theory is that occlusive dressings such as silicone gel sheets increase the temperature of the scar and thereby collagenase activity. They are worn 12 to 24 hours a day for up to a year. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 16"},{"q":"A 44-year-old woman with a history of plaque psoriasis was given a two-week course of oral prednisone by an urgent care clinician for back pain. Four days after finishing it she develops fever lasting several days followed by the sudden appearance of widespread sheets of small pustules on erythematous skin. She appears unwell. What has most likely occurred?","choices":["Impetigo herpetiformis","Acute generalised exanthematous pustulosis from the prednisone","A widespread bacterial skin infection","Guttate psoriasis triggered by the steroid course","Pustular psoriasis precipitated by systemic steroid withdrawal"],"answer":4,"exp":"Correct. Pustular psoriasis of von Zumbusch type is a rare, severe form associated with other forms of psoriasis and may follow systemic steroid withdrawal. It is abrupt and life-threatening, begins with several days of fever, and is commonly mistaken for bacterial or viral infection. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 160"},{"q":"A 6-month-old girl is brought to the clinic by her father for a rash that has been present for three weeks. He reports she is difficult to settle at night and rubs her face against the bedsheet rather than scratching it. Her father has allergic rhinitis and her older brother has asthma. On examination there are weeping inflammatory patches and crusted plaques over both cheeks and the extensor surfaces of the forearms. The antecubital and popliteal fossae are clear. There is no fever, no purulent drainage, and no history of new detergents, soaps, or foods. What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Atopic dermatitis, childhood pattern","Atopic dermatitis, infantile pattern","Irritant contact dermatitis","Allergic contact dermatitis","Seborrheic dermatitis"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. In infants atopic dermatitis produces weeping inflammatory patches and crusted plaques on the cheeks, scalp, and extensor surfaces, and rubbing commonly replaces scratching at this age. The family history of allergic rhinitis and asthma completes the atopic triad, and roughly 80 percent of patients have a personal or family history of atopy. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 54"},{"q":"A 4-year-old girl has a typical history and examination for atopic dermatitis. Her parents ask whether blood tests are needed to confirm the diagnosis. What is the most accurate response?","choices":["Check an immunoglobulin E level in every child","It is clinical; no routine laboratory testing","Culture every child at diagnosis","Patch test every child at diagnosis","Biopsy every child before treatment"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The diagnosis is usually clinical, supported by family history, personal history of atopy, and a recurrent history of rash. Immunoglobulin E may be elevated but is not routinely tested, and routine laboratory testing is not required. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 56"},{"q":"A 32-year-old woman has an asymptomatic ring of flesh-coloured to erythematous papules on the dorsum of her hand that has slowly enlarged over months. There is no scale. A potassium hydroxide preparation is negative. What finding on punch biopsy would confirm the diagnosis?","choices":["Palisading granulomas with central necrobiosis","Band-like lymphocytes in the upper dermis","Septal panniculitis without vasculitis","Dermal neutrophils without organisms","Hyphae in the stratum corneum"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Granuloma annulare shows palisading granulomas with central necrobiosis and mucin deposition, reflecting a type IV delayed hypersensitivity reaction forming granulomas around degenerating collagen. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 51"},{"q":"A 38-year-old man has a blotchy eruption on the trunk whose edges cannot be traced, and the clinician documents it as poorly demarcated. What does this term convey?","choices":["Irregular, blotchy borders that are not well defined","Elevated above the surrounding skin","It changes when pressure is applied","Clear borders but irregular internal colour","Smaller than 1 cm"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. A poorly demarcated lesion has an irregular or blotchy appearance without well-defined borders, in contrast to a well-demarcated lesion, which has clearly defined borders. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 20"},{"q":"A 9-year-old girl with red hair has multiple small light brown symmetric macules 3 to 5 mm across her nose and cheeks. Her mother reports they darken every summer and fade in winter. What is the inheritance pattern, and what treatment is specifically not used?","choices":["Somatic mutation; excision is first line","Autosomal recessive; cryotherapy is first line","X-linked; response is equal in all skin types","Autosomal dominant; one treatment clears them","Autosomal dominant; cryotherapy is not used"],"answer":4,"exp":"Correct. Ephelides, or freckles, are autosomal dominant and appear most commonly on sun-exposed skin of fair-skinned individuals, often with blonde or red hair. They become more pronounced in spring and summer and fade in winter. Cryotherapy is not used because it is difficult given the size of the lesions. — CMS I Pigmented Skin Lesions - Shahsv-2.pptx, Slide 7"},{"q":"A clinician estimates the likelihood of a condition in a 60-year-old woman before ordering a test, then revises that estimate once the result returns. How do pretest and posttest probability differ?","choices":["They are the same quantity","Pretest is after the result, posttest before","Pretest applies only to screening","Pretest is before the result, posttest after","Posttest ignores the clinical picture"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Pretest probability is the likelihood of a condition before the test result is known, informed by signs and symptoms, history and risk factors, and how common the condition is in the population. Posttest probability is the likelihood after the result is known. — 1. svClinical Reasoning and Problem Solving.pptx, Slide 9"},{"q":"A 52-year-old woman bitten by a brown recluse spider four days ago has a darkening lesion on the calf. How does a brown recluse bite evolve in the small percentage of cases that progress, and what governs the timing of surgery?","choices":["Painless induration with vesicles by 36 hours","Necrosis at 2 to 3 days, eschar by day 7; delay surgery","Systemic symptoms within 30 minutes","Resolution without tissue loss","Full-thickness necrosis within hours; debride now"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. In a small percentage of cases the initial wound progresses to necrosis 2 to 3 days after the bite, then eschar formation between days 5 and 7, then deep ulcers. Necrotic wounds heal very slowly and may need surgical intervention or reconstruction, with surgery delayed. — CMS I Dermatological Infestations - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 62"},{"q":"A 63-year-old man has multiple light to dark brown macules with well-defined but irregular borders that tend to coalesce over his shoulders, where he has had severe sunburns. What is the underlying process and its epidemiological association?","choices":["Dermal capillary proliferation","Basal melanocyte proliferation with more melanin","Somatic mutation present from birth","T cell destruction of melanocytes","Hyperinsulinaemia driving keratinocyte growth"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Solar lentigines arise from proliferation of basal melanocytes with increased melanin production and are strongly associated with older age, present in about 90 percent by age 50, along with sun damage, tanning, a history of ephelides, and birth control use. — CMS I Pigmented Skin Lesions - Shahsv-2.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"A 68-year-old woman describes three days of burning pain in a band across her left flank, followed by an eruption. Examination shows grouped vesicles on an erythematous base confined to a single band that stops abruptly at the midline. What are the three clinical phases of this condition?","choices":["Incubation, contagious, and latency","Pre-eruptive, eruptive, and scarring","Pre-eruptive, acute eruptive, postherpetic neuralgia","Prodromal fever, generalised eruption, desquamation","Erythematous, bullous, and necrotic"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The pre-eruptive phase is characterised by dysesthesia or pain within the affected dermatome with lesion onset by 48 to 72 hours; the acute eruptive phase produces grouped herpetiform vesicles on an erythematous base with new lesions forming over 3 to 5 days; and postherpetic neuralgia is the chronic phase. — 6. Fungal and Viral Skin Infections - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 97"},{"q":"A 25-year-old woman with several common warts on the hands asks whether treatment will get rid of the virus for good. What treatment principle applies to all cutaneous warts?","choices":["Cryotherapy eradicates it and prevents recurrence","No therapy eradicates the virus; recurrence happens","Biopsy every wart first","Excise deeply; they reach the dermis","Treat all warts at diagnosis"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. No therapy eradicates human papillomavirus with certainty and recurrence can occur. Treatment is chosen according to location, symptoms, age, pregnancy status, immune status, and the risk of scarring or dyspigmentation, avoiding excessive freezing or destruction. — 6. Fungal and Viral Skin Infections - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 149"},{"q":"A 51-year-old woman with known rosacea reports a gritty foreign-body sensation, blurred vision, and photophobia. What is the most appropriate action?","choices":["Reassure the patient that ocular symptoms are unrelated to rosacea","Urgent ophthalmology referral","Begin oral isotretinoin","Routine ophthalmology referral within three months","Begin topical metronidazole and reassess in six weeks"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Ocular rosacea with keratitis, visual symptoms, or corneal involvement warrants urgent ophthalmology referral. Visual symptoms and photophobia raise exactly those concerns. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 70"},{"q":"A 40-year-old man has a painful fluctuant nodule 4 mm across on the back of the neck. He is afebrile and it is a single lesion. What is the most appropriate management?","choices":["Warm compresses plus oral cephalexin","Oral trimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole","Immediate incision and drainage","Warm compresses without antibiotics","Squeezing the lesion"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Warm compresses are usually sufficient for small furuncles, and no antibiotics are given if the patient is afebrile with a single lesion under 5 mm. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 65"},{"q":"A 25-year-old woman with a history of keloids asks about surgical removal of a keloid on her shoulder. What should she be told?","choices":["Excise only after a confirmatory biopsy","Excision alone is usually curative","Silicone sheeting for one week first","Excision alone recurs in 50 to 100 percent","Cryotherapy instead, with no adverse effects"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Surgical removal has a 50 to 100 percent recurrence rate and the recurrence is often larger than the original lesion, which is why excision is followed with intralesional steroid. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 18"},{"q":"A 9-year-old boy has scaly patches of hair loss, and the clinician suspects a fungal or bacterial infection producing pigment change and reaches for a handheld device emitting long-wave ultraviolet light. What is this device and what does it evaluate?","choices":["A transilluminator, separating solid from fluid","A trichoscope, magnifying the scalp","A phototherapy unit delivering ultraviolet B","A Wood's lamp, evaluating pigment change","A dermoscope, magnifying under polarised light"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. A Wood's lamp is a handheld diagnostic device emitting long-wave ultraviolet light to highlight subtle changes in the skin, scalp, and hair, and it evaluates pigment changes in selected fungal or bacterial infections. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 27"},{"q":"A 40-year-old man is found to have onychomycosis of three toenails and a coexisting tinea pedis. He asks whether a cream will clear both. What is the most accurate response?","choices":["Inadequate for both; systemic needed for each","Adequate for the skin, inadequate for the nails","Add a topical corticosteroid to both","Neither site needs treatment","Adequate for both skin and nails"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Topical antifungals are particularly effective against dermatophytes, but topical therapy is inadequate for most scalp or nail infections. The skin disease will respond while the nails require systemic treatment. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 43"},{"q":"A 33-year-old woman presents with widespread itchy wheals that have been appearing and disappearing for three days. Individual lesions last a few hours, blanch fully, and leave no mark. There is no fever, no joint pain, and no bruising at resolved sites. What is the most appropriate first-line treatment?","choices":["Oral prednisone for five days","A second-generation antihistamine","Intramuscular epinephrine","A first-generation antihistamine","Skin biopsy before treatment"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Second-generation antihistamines are first-line for urticaria. Urticaria results from mast cell degranulation releasing histamine, prostaglandins, and leukotrienes, causing transient dermal oedema. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 39"},{"q":"An 83-year-old nursing home resident with limited mobility and incontinence is at risk of pressure injury. What is the single best measure?","choices":["Referral for surgical debridement","Restricting fluids","Prophylactic hydrocolloid dressings","Repositioning every eight hours","Prevention, with repositioning every two hours"],"answer":4,"exp":"Correct. The best measure is prevention: frequent skin assessments, nutrition assessment, moisture control and skin care with the skin kept clean and dry, incontinence managed and barrier creams applied, and repositioning every two hours. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 35"},{"q":"An 8-year-old boy has three smooth, non-scarring patches of hair loss on the scalp with exclamation point hairs at the margins and fine pitting of several fingernails. A potassium hydroxide preparation of plucked hairs is negative. What is the most appropriate first-line treatment?","choices":["Intralesional corticosteroids","Oral prednisone","Topical minoxidil","Oral terbinafine","Topical corticosteroids"],"answer":4,"exp":"Correct. Topical steroids are first line in children 10 years old and younger. The child is 8, which places him in that group and avoids the discomfort of intralesional injection in a young child. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 140"},{"q":"A 44-year-old man has a purulent collection in the axilla at the site of an old puncture wound, and a student asks how this differs from a furuncle. How does a cutaneous abscess differ in origin from a furuncle?","choices":["Abscess from a follicle; furuncle from inoculation","Abscess from inoculation; furuncle from a follicle","Abscess involves apocrine, furuncle eccrine glands","Abscess is sterile; furuncle infected","Abscess is superficial; furuncle epidermal"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. An abscess is a collection of purulent material within the dermis and deeper tissues, often due to traumatic inoculation of bacteria into the skin, compared with furuncles, which arise from infected hair follicles. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 113"},{"q":"A 27-year-old woman has an itchy papular eruption two days after swimming in a freshwater lake. What is the management of cercarial dermatitis?","choices":["Whole-body overnight permethrin","Oral albendazole for 3 days","Extermination of the water source","Antihistamines, oatmeal baths, and antipruritic lotions","Doxycycline for 5 to 10 days"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Management is symptomatic: antihistamines, oatmeal baths, antipruritic lotions, aspirin for pain control, proper washing and hygiene, and topical or oral glucocorticoids. — CMS I Dermatological Infestations - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 91"},{"q":"A 24-year-old man presents six hours after a day at the beach with painful erythema, warmth, and tenderness across the shoulders and back. There is no blistering. What is the most appropriate acute management?","choices":["Immediate systemic corticosteroids","Liberal topical anaesthetics","Prophylactic oral antibiotics","Cool compresses, early anti-inflammatories, hydration","Cold immersion and delayed anti-inflammatories"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Acute management is cool compresses and cool rather than cold water immersion, non-steroidal anti-inflammatory drugs started early to reduce prostaglandin-mediated inflammation and pain, oral hydration, and topical moisturisers. First-degree sunburn resolves in 3 to 5 days with desquamation. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 96"},{"q":"A 23-year-old woman has plantar warts that are not painful and do not interfere with walking. What is the appropriate management?","choices":["Oral antiviral therapy to eradicate human papillomavirus","Daily salicylic acid 40 percent indefinitely to prevent recurrence","Surgical excision to remove the roots","No therapy is required unless they are painful","Cryotherapy every 2 to 3 weeks regardless of symptoms"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Plantar warts do not require therapy unless they are painful. They can cluster together to form a mosaic wart, and treatment when needed is salicylic acid 40 percent or cryotherapy. — 6. Fungal and Viral Skin Infections - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 145"},{"q":"A 34-year-old man with untreated human immunodeficiency virus infection has multiple red-purple macules and plaques on the trunk. What examination is described as essential, and what is the first management priority?","choices":["Oral examination; start chemotherapy first","Ophthalmological examination; intralesional vincristine","Lymph node examination; observation","Oral examination; start or optimise antiretroviral therapy","Skin examination alone; mucosa is spared"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Oral examination is essential when Kaposi sarcoma is suspected, since hard palate lesions are common and may be the presenting site. For acquired immunodeficiency syndrome-associated disease the first priority is to begin or optimise antiretroviral therapy, because immune restoration is the cornerstone of treatment. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 66"},{"q":"A 47-year-old man presents with a new complaint and the clinician must decide how to proceed with him. What three questions must clinicians answer in clinical decision making?","choices":["What disease, what prognosis, what cost","Pretest, posttest, and likelihood ratio","Which guideline, which specialist, when to follow up","Is the patient stable, certain, and informed","What disease, should we test, should we treat"],"answer":4,"exp":"Correct. Clinical decision making requires answering what disease the patient has, whether testing should be done, and whether this patient should be treated — alongside deciding what information to gather and how to integrate it. — 1. svClinical Reasoning and Problem Solving.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"A student choosing between two tests for a 44-year-old patient asks which error each test property guards against. Which statement correctly pairs each test property with the error it reduces?","choices":["Sensitivity is for screening, specificity for diagnosis","Sensitivity reduces false negatives; specificity false positives","Neither relates to false results","Both reduce false negatives equally","Sensitivity reduces false positives; specificity false negatives"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Sensitivity describes how well a test detects a condition and is related to fewer false negatives. Specificity is the ability to correctly identify patients who do not have the disease and is related to fewer false positives. — 1. svClinical Reasoning and Problem Solving.pptx, Slide 8"},{"q":"A 41-year-old man has a moveable nodule on the back with a central punctum, which he refers to as a sebaceous cyst. Why is that term a misnomer for an epidermoid cyst?","choices":["It arises from apocrine glands","It is filled with joint mucin","It is enclosed epithelium filled with keratin","It has no lining, being a pseudo-cyst","It contains lipid-laden macrophages"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. An epidermoid cyst is formed by cystic enclosure of epithelium within the dermis and becomes filled with keratin. It is often called a sebaceous cyst because it appears to contain sebum. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 53"},{"q":"A 24-year-old man has painful grouped vesicles on an erythematous base on the buttock that have broken down into shallow ulcers. He asks whether the location tells him which virus type he has. What is the correct response?","choices":["Oral is always type 1, genital always type 2","The prodrome identifies the type","Genital lesions are always type 2","Either type causes either site; location is unreliable","The vesicle appearance identifies the type"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Either herpes simplex virus type can cause oral or genital infection, and lesion location does not reliably determine type. Infections can occur anywhere on the skin, and typing requires a type-specific test. — 6. Fungal and Viral Skin Infections - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 112"},{"q":"A 34-year-old man presents with recurrent pruritic scaling of the scalp, eyebrows, nasolabial folds, and external ears. Examination demonstrates greasy, yellow-white scale over mildly erythematous, poorly demarcated patches. He reports the itching is worse when he sweats. What is the most appropriate initial treatment?","choices":["Oral doxycycline","A high-potency topical corticosteroid","Oral terbinafine","Topical mupirocin","Ketoconazole shampoo and cream"],"answer":4,"exp":"Correct. Seborrheic dermatitis is caused by overgrowth of Malassezia, a normal skin flora organism, so topical antifungals are the mainstay. Antifungal shampoos such as ketoconazole and selenium sulfide are used on the scalp and antifungal creams or lotions on the face. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 95"},{"q":"A 27-year-old man develops widespread wheals within 20 minutes of eating shellfish. What mechanism underlies this reaction, and what class of trigger does it represent?","choices":["Immune complex deposition","Complement deficiency","Immunoglobulin E mediated, an immunologic trigger","Immunoglobulin E mediated, a non-immunologic trigger","A delayed type IV reaction"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Urticaria results from mast cell degranulation releasing histamine, prostaglandins, and leukotrienes, producing transient dermal oedema. Immunologic immunoglobulin E mediated triggers include foods such as shellfish, nuts, and eggs, drugs including penicillin, and insect stings. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 36"},{"q":"A 21-year-old man treated for pityriasis versicolor returns two months later frustrated that the pale patches persist despite his completing the full course. What is the most accurate explanation?","choices":["Pigment recovery lags months behind clearance","Vitiligo is developing at the sites","A secondary bacterial infection","It is treatment failure needing oral therapy","It is now contagious to the household"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Hypopigmentation reflects altered melanocyte function and reduced tanning, and recovery can lag months after yeast clearance. The persistence of pallor does not indicate treatment failure. — 6. Fungal and Viral Skin Infections - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 77"},{"q":"A 33-year-old woman has a suspected skin allergy, and the clinician must choose between the two allergy tests available in the clinic. What is the correct pairing?","choices":["Both detect delayed type IV reactions","Both are performed on blood samples","Patch testing for contact dermatitis; prick for immediate","Patch detects fungi; prick detects bacteria","Prick testing for contact dermatitis; patch for immediate"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Patch testing diagnoses allergic contact dermatitis, a delayed type IV reaction, while skin-prick testing evaluates immediate, immunoglobulin E mediated allergic reactions. Immunoglobulin E allergy testing evaluates such allergies in the blood. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 35"},{"q":"A 38-year-old man in Oregon has a painless bite on the forearm that has become indurated with a large surrounding erythematous area. In which region of the United States is the hobo spider the predominant cause of necrotic arachnidism, and what is the early course of its bite?","choices":["Pacific Northwest; immediate severe pain","Northeast; erythema migrans","Pacific Northwest; a painless bite","Southwest; crampy abdominal pain","Midwest and Southeast; the red, white, and blue sign"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The hobo spider, also called the aggressive house spider, is the predominant cause of necrotic arachnidism in the Pacific Northwest. The bite is painless, with induration and paraesthesia of the bite site within 30 minutes, a large erythematous area, and vesicle formation during the first 36 hours. — CMS I Dermatological Infestations - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 64"},{"q":"A 28-year-old woman is prescribed topical permethrin for scabies. What counselling should be given about the response to treatment?","choices":["Weekly for three months","Twenty minutes then rinse; no other measures","To visible areas only; resolves in 24 hours","Overnight to all skin; itch may persist for weeks","Overnight to all skin; persistent itch means failure"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Topical permethrin is applied overnight to the entire skin surface with special attention to creases. Most patients experience relief in about 3 days, but rash and pruritus may persist afterwards, which patients must be warned about or they will assume treatment failure. — CMS I Dermatological Infestations - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 19"},{"q":"A 68-year-old woman with herpes zoster asks whether her unvaccinated 4-year-old grandson can catch shingles from her. What is the most accurate explanation?","choices":["There is no transmission risk at all","Transmission is by respiratory droplets only","He can catch shingles from the lesions","Only if he has never had chickenpox","He cannot catch shingles, but could catch chickenpox"],"answer":4,"exp":"Correct. A susceptible contact does not catch shingles. Herpes zoster is reactivation of latent virus in that individual, but exposure to vesicular fluid or, in disseminated disease, airborne virus can transmit varicella-zoster virus to a susceptible person and cause varicella. — 6. Fungal and Viral Skin Infections - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 91"},{"q":"A 54-year-old woman is found on routine examination to have several smooth, firm, deep red papules under 5 mm on the trunk that blanch with pressure. She has never noticed them. What education should be given?","choices":["They indicate liver disease","Biopsy is needed to diagnose them","Benign, but can progress to malignancy","Benign capillary proliferation; more will appear","Caused by increased melanocytes"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Cherry angiomas are acquired vascular lesions formed by capillary or venule proliferation, very common and increasing with age. Treatment is unnecessary unless they bother the patient, and patients should be aware that new lesions will likely develop and there is no way to prevent them. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 87"},{"q":"A 79-year-old man has severe xerosis of both lower legs and a barrier preparation is being chosen for him. What consideration should guide the choice of vehicle?","choices":["Avoid occlusive vehicles in xerosis","Lotions are the most occlusive","Ointments are more occlusive than creams or lotions","Vehicle matters only for corticosteroids","Creams and ointments are equally occlusive"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Emollients and barrier preparations are the foundation of treatment for many inflammatory conditions, and ointments are generally more occlusive than creams or lotions. Common uses include xerosis, eczema, irritant dermatitis, and diaper dermatitis. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 40"},{"q":"A 62-year-old woman has several uniformly tan-brown macules on the dorsal hands and face that have appeared gradually over years. One lesion is larger, asymmetric, and has irregular borders with darker areas within it. What should be done about that lesion?","choices":["Apply a topical retinoid and review in six months","Treat it as a seborrheic keratosis and leave it alone","Treat it as a café-au-lait macule and observe","Reassure her that it is a solar lentigo like the others","Evaluate it with dermoscopy for lentigo maligna"],"answer":4,"exp":"Correct. Lentigo maligna, which is melanoma in situ, is asymmetric with irregular border and colour and darker areas, and dermoscopy is described as essential. A solar lentigo that has diverged from its uniform neighbours requires that evaluation. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 111"},{"q":"A 42-year-old woman has central facial papules and pustules, and the clinician looks for the one lesion type that would settle whether this is acne. What is the hallmark lesion of acne vulgaris, and why does it matter diagnostically?","choices":["The nodule, whose presence defines the diagnosis","The papule, which is unique to acne among facial eruptions","The telangiectasia, whose presence confirms acne","The comedone, whose absence argues against acne and toward rosacea","The pustule, whose absence argues against acne"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Comedones are the hallmark of acne vulgaris, and the differential specifically distinguishes rosacea by papules and pustules in the middle third of the face with telangiectasias, flushing, and an absence of comedones. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"A 30-year-old woman has intensely itchy finger-web lesions and a scraping is taken from a burrow. What is the definitive method of diagnosing scabies, and what dermoscopic finding supports it?","choices":["Serology for anti-mite antibodies","Microscopy plus a positive Nikolsky sign","A burrow ink test alone","Microscopy of the mite, ova, or feces","Potassium hydroxide showing spaghetti and meatballs"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Definitive diagnosis is by microscopic identification of the organism, ova, or feces, obtained by skin scraping. The classic dermoscopic finding is the delta-wing jet sign, representing the dense scabies head, body, eggs, and burrow. — CMS I Dermatological Infestations - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 14"},{"q":"A 40-year-old man in Connecticut develops an expanding erythematous oval lesion 8 cm across with central clearing and a darker punctate centre at the site of a recent tick bite. What stage is this, what is the lesion called, and what should be done about testing?","choices":["Stage 2; intravenous ceftriaxone","Stage 1; wait for two-tier serology","Cellulitis; cephalexin","Stage 3; look for arthritis","Stage 1 erythema migrans; treat clinically"],"answer":4,"exp":"Correct. Stage 1 early localised infection is characterised by erythema migrans, a lesion greater than 5 cm that expands with central clearing and often a darker punctate centre, also called a bull's-eye. A patient with this lesion should be diagnosed and treated on that basis, and testing is most helpful in patients from non-endemic regions with non-diagnostic symptoms. — CMS I Dermatological Infestations - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 72"},{"q":"A 62-year-old fair-skinned man who always burns and never tans has a solitary, smooth, shiny, dome-shaped red papule with a central keratin plug on his forearm that appeared and grew rapidly over seven weeks. What is the only reliable way to make the diagnosis, and why does it matter?","choices":["Clinical appearance alone","Observation for 3 to 6 months","Biopsy","Dermoscopy","A potassium hydroxide preparation"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Biopsy is the only reliable method to make the diagnosis. Squamous cell carcinoma heads the differential, and the standard of care is to excise or destroy the tumour precisely because of possible malignancy. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 51"}]};
